artifact.self_contained_problem:
Work in the Euclidean plane $\mathbb{R}^2$. A finite embedded geodesic net is a finite connected graph $G$ embedded in $\mathbb{R}^2$ such that:
- every edge is a straight line segment of multiplicity one;
- no two edges cross or meet except at common endpoints (no self-intersections);
- each vertex $v$ is classified as either balanced or unbalanced, where $v$ is balanced if the sum of the unit tangent vectors along all edges of $G$ incident to $v$ (pointing away from $v$) equals the zero vector; otherwise $v$ is unbalanced;
- no edge has both endpoints unbalanced.

Question: For every integer $B \geq 1$, does there exist such a finite embedded geodesic net having exactly four unbalanced vertices and at least $B$ balanced vertices? Moreover, can this be achieved with the four unbalanced vertices fixed to be the four vertices of a square in $\mathbb{R}^2$?

